  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The specimen gives a clear envelope-level answer to filename loss by carrying an explicit pCID next to the payload, so interpretation does not depend on local paths. But the scenario is really about CAS object type binding, and this draft mostly delegates raw-chunk-versus-pointer semantics to the payload protocol rather than defining them here.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit pCID dispatch binds payload interpretation to transported bytes instead of filenames.",
      "Deterministic CBOR positional encoding gives stable bytes for hashing, comparison, and later audit.",
      "Unknown pCIDs fail visibly and best-effort salvage must be marked unsupported and unverified."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The draft does not itself specify how standalone CAS bytes are distinguished as raw chunks versus pointer objects.",
      "It is unclear how Bob recognizes the outer object as a grid envelope from CID/codec information alone.",
      "Integrity and authorship depend on separate wrapper protocols rather than a built-in slot."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Best-effort inspection of unknown payloads could still be over-trusted in practice despite the warning labels.",
      "Referenced or extracted inner CAS objects may remain ambiguous if their own protocols do not bind raw versus pointer meaning.",
      "The specimen is still draft and unfrozen, so long-term interoperability details may change."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Should raw chunks and pointer objects use distinct pCIDs, or be distinguished inside one payload schema?",
      "What CID codec or other durable cue tells a peer to parse opaque bytes as this outer grid envelope at all?",
      "How will future peers recover the relevant pCID/spec material for decades-scale audit and replay?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
